       "target": "org[1][1]",
       "delay": 0.4
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "With many cores, one memory becomes a bottleneck. So memory is split up, with a slice beside each node. A core reaches its own slice quickly, and others' slices over a network, more slowly. Non-uniform memory access, NUMA, or distributed shared memory."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "org[0][2]"
      },
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "org[1][2]",
       "delay": 0.4
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "UMA suits a few to a few dozen cores, like one multicore chip. NUMA scales to hundreds, like multi-socket servers. Either way, the programmer sees one shared memory."
     },
     "do": [
      {
       "action": "highlight",
       "target": "org[2..=3]"
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "say": {
      "en": "On NUMA, where data lives matters. If 80 percent of a thread's misses go to its local memory at 100 nanoseconds, and 20 percent to a remote node at 300, the average is 140. Operating systems try to place each page near the thread that uses it."
     },
     "do": [
      {
